WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.624 --> 00:00:10.617
It was September 1952, and eight-year-old Anna told her mother she wasn't feeling well.

2
00:00:10.618 --> 00:00:16.106
When the little girl came home from school, her right leg was dragging and she had a horrible headache.

3
00:00:16.107 --> 00:00:21.557
By that evening, Anna's legs were paralyzed, and she couldn't lift her head off her pillow.

4
00:00:21.558 --> 00:00:28.881
Anna had polio, also known as infantile paralysis, an infectious disease that primarily affects children.

5
00:00:28.882 --> 00:00:37.716
Polio is caused by a virus which attacks the nerve cells that control the muscles in the body, resulting in loss of movement and sometimes even death.

6
00:00:37.717 --> 00:00:48.037
But only a few years after Anna got sick, Jonas Salk, an American physician and medical scientist, became the first person to create a vaccine to prevent polio.

7
00:00:48.038 --> 00:00:55.148
Son of Russian-Jewish immigrants, Jonas Salk was born on October 28, 1914, in New York City.

8
00:00:55.149 --> 00:01:05.468
Two years later, America was experiencing the worst polio epidemic in the country's history, and Salk's parents, both factory workers, were frightened for their little boy.

9
00:01:05.469 --> 00:01:12.460
In 1916, there were nine thousand polio cases in New York City, and people were in a panic.

10
00:01:12.461 --> 00:01:19.904
Cardboard signs bearing the words "INFANTILE PARALYSIS" started appearing on the doors of apartments and houses in the city.

11
00:01:19.905 --> 00:01:25.798
The signs warned everyone not to go in or come out, by order of the board of health.

12
00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:30.796
Thousands of people fled the city, and those that couldn't kept their children inside.

13
00:01:30.797 --> 00:01:36.763
As a child, Salk preferred to stay home and read, rather than go outside to play with friends.

14
00:01:36.764 --> 00:01:43.436
He attended Townsend Harris High School, a school for gifted children, and graduated at age 15.

15
00:01:43.437 --> 00:01:51.874
At first, he wanted to pursue a law career, but after he took some biology courses at college, he decided to become a physician instead.

16
00:01:51.875 --> 00:02:01.368
Later, Jonas Salk became interested in viruses—tiny organisms that replicate themselves inside living things and cause infections, such as polio.

17
00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:13.516
In 1942, when Salk was in his last year of medical school at New York University College of Medicine, his work on the recently discovered influenza virus led him to a new idea about vaccines.

18
00:02:13.517 --> 00:02:22.240
Vaccines are preventive medicines made from a substance containing a form of the germs (in this case, viruses) that cause a particular disease.

19
00:02:22.241 --> 00:02:31.677
When this substance is injected into the human body, antibodies are produced that fight the virus and create immunity to the disease caused by that virus.

20
00:02:31.678 --> 00:02:41.005
Traditionally, vaccines were made from live forms of a particular virus because scientists believed that only live viruses could create antibodies.

21
00:02:41.006 --> 00:02:49.862
Unfortunately, in some cases, the live-virus vaccine did not create immunity or prevent the disease—it caused the disease.

22
00:02:49.863 --> 00:02:52.009
This fact concerned Salk.

23
00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:58.548
He said, "I just questioned the logic of it, the reasonableness of it, when other people accepted it."

24
00:02:58.549 --> 00:03:04.209
Salk's idea was to use a killed virus instead, which could never cause the disease.

25
00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:09.907
Although Salk was confident his approach would work, most scientists did not agree with him.

26
00:03:09.908 --> 00:03:19.311
After graduation from medical school, Salk joined a research team headed by a former professor and mentor, who also believed in using killed-virus vaccines.

27
00:03:19.312 --> 00:03:25.805
The team developed a successful, killed-virus flu vaccine that was administered to soldiers during World War II.

28
00:03:25.806 --> 00:03:33.675
In 1947, Salk left his mentor to become the head of the virus-research lab at the University of Pittsburgh.

29
00:03:33.676 --> 00:03:50.065
The next year, he was asked to assist with a project funded by the National Foundation for Infantile Paralysis (also called the March of Dimes), an organization founded in 1938 by President Franklin Delano Roosevelt, himself a polio victim.

30
00:03:50.066 --> 00:03:59.551
Then, people contributed to the March of Dimes campaign by sending their dimes to the White House to help fight polio, which affected about fifty thousand Americans each year.

31
00:03:59.552 --> 00:04:05.453
By 1950, Salk was deeply involved in the development of a vaccine to conquer polio.

32
00:04:05.454 --> 00:04:13.595
As an experiment, he injected the killed virus into monkeys to see if the vaccine was safe and would trigger the production of antibodies.

33
00:04:13.596 --> 00:04:16.386
His experiment was a success.

34
00:04:16.387 --> 00:04:27.158
Next, in September 1952, Salk vaccinated children who already suffered from polio to determine if the number of antibodies within their bodies could be increased.

35
00:04:27.159 --> 00:04:36.620
The answer was yes, and Salk later said, "That was the first evidence that we were able to do in humans what we could do in animals."

36
00:04:36.621 --> 00:04:42.971
Salk was convinced the vaccine would be safe and effective, but more testing was needed to be sure.

37
00:04:42.972 --> 00:04:54.404
Unfortunately, the press learned of Salk's early success with the vaccine, and in 1953, headlines falsely proclaimed that a polio vaccine would soon be available to the public.

38
00:04:54.405 --> 00:05:00.340
The country reacted with such joy that Salk felt he needed to clear up the misunderstanding.

39
00:05:00.341 --> 00:05:04.726
Salk appeared on a radio program to explain the actual situation.

40
00:05:04.727 --> 00:05:14.274
He said, "Although progress has been more rapid than we had any right to expect, there will be no vaccine available . . . for the next polio season."

41
00:05:14.275 --> 00:05:19.107
The scientific community saw Salk's radio appearance as a publicity stunt.

42
00:05:19.108 --> 00:05:25.795
One scientist said, "Jonas went on the air that night to take a bow and become a public hero."

43
00:05:25.796 --> 00:05:35.415
In addition, despite Salk's successful polio vaccine experiments, most scientists still doubted a killed-virus vaccine could be effective.

44
00:05:35.416 --> 00:05:45.568
During this time, an older and better-known scientist, Albert Sabin, was working on a live-virus polio vaccine, and the competition between the two men was fierce.

45
00:05:45.569 --> 00:05:53.578
After Salk's radio appearance, Sabin constantly belittled his rival in public, but the criticism didn't stop Salk's research.

46
00:05:53.579 --> 00:06:03.327
On April 12, 1955, an announcement was made that Salk's polio vaccine was finally ready, and Salk was hailed all over the world.

47
00:06:03.328 --> 00:06:10.113
In the next four years, over 450 million doses of Salk's vaccine were given to children.

48
00:06:10.114 --> 00:06:21.106
But the controversy over which vaccine was best did not end, because the Sabin vaccine, which came out a few years later, had some advantages over the Salk vaccine.

49
00:06:21.107 --> 00:06:27.273
Sabin's vaccine did not need refrigeration and could be administered orally on a sugar cube.

50
00:06:27.274 --> 00:06:37.803
Since this vaccine, unlike Salk's, did not have to be injected, it could be given by nonmedical personnel, making it suitable for use in remote communities around the world.

51
00:06:37.804 --> 00:06:48.349
So, in 1961, Albert Sabin's live-virus vaccine was approved for wide use in the United States and was provided free to all American schoolchildren.

52
00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:57.848
Even though Jonas Salk was the first researcher to develop the polio vaccine, he never sought a patent for his discovery and never made money from it.

53
00:06:57.849 --> 00:07:02.577
When a television newsman asked him, "Who owns the patent on this vaccine?"

54
00:07:02.578 --> 00:07:10.172
Salk said simply, "Well, the people, I would say. There is no patent. Could you patent the sun?"

55
00:07:10.173 --> 00:07:27.882
Although Salk did not receive science's greatest honor, the Nobel Prize, partly because of his problems with the scientific community, he was awarded America's two highest civilian honors: the Congressional Gold Medal in 1955 and the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 1977.

56
00:07:27.883 --> 00:07:38.831
In 1963, Jonas Salk realized a dream when he opened the Salk Institute for Biological Studies, a place for other scientists to conduct research and teach.

57
00:07:38.832 --> 00:07:43.862
In 1990, Salk and the institute began work on a vaccine for AIDS.

58
00:07:43.863 --> 00:07:59.085
After Salk's death of heart failure on June 23, 1995, Dr. Francis Crick, co-discoverer of the DNA molecule, said of Jonas Salk, "Few have made one discovery that has benefited humanity so greatly."

59
00:07:59.086 --> 00:08:07.681
Jonas Salk became known as "The Man Who Saved the Children," because he prevented so many children from falling victim to this crippling disease.

60
00:08:07.682 --> 00:08:19.211
In 2001, there were only 483 reported cases of polio globally, but scientists remain vigilant as efforts continue to create a polio-free world.
